<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Linux on Testing Observation</title><link>https://chengxiaqiucao.github.io/en/tags/linux/</link><description>Recent content in Linux on Testing Observation</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en</language><copyright>|by Daniel Wei</copyright><lastBuildDate>Thu, 13 Mar 2025 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://chengxiaqiucao.github.io/en/tags/linux/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>A Guide to sed, One of the Linux Text Processing Trio</title><link>https://chengxiaqiucao.github.io/en/post/061-linux%E6%96%87%E6%9C%AC%E5%A4%84%E7%90%86%E4%B8%89%E5%89%91%E5%AE%A2-sed/</link><pubDate>Thu, 13 Mar 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://chengxiaqiucao.github.io/en/post/061-linux%E6%96%87%E6%9C%AC%E5%A4%84%E7%90%86%E4%B8%89%E5%89%91%E5%AE%A2-sed/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://chengxiaqiucao.github.io/post/061-linux%E6%96%87%E6%9C%AC%E5%A4%84%E7%90%86%E4%B8%89%E5%89%91%E5%AE%A2-sed/sed3.webp" alt="Featured image of post A Guide to sed, One of the Linux Text Processing Trio" />&lt;h1 id="preface">Preface
&lt;/h1>&lt;p>In the previous article in this series 【】, we introduced the usage of the &lt;code>grep&lt;/code> command, one of the Linux Text Processing Trio. But in reality, among the trio, &lt;code>grep&lt;/code> is really just the little brother: enhanced by regular expressions, its role is mostly to perform pattern queries against text.
The truly heavyweight commands in the trio are &lt;code>sed&lt;/code> and &lt;code>awk&lt;/code>. &lt;code>sed&lt;/code> is actually short for &lt;code>stream editor&lt;/code>, so the sed command can genuinely function as a text editor. In this article, we&amp;rsquo;ll introduce this &lt;strong>editor disguised as a Linux command&lt;/strong>: &lt;code>sed&lt;/code>&lt;/p>
&lt;h1 id="overview-of-the-sed-command">Overview of the sed Command
&lt;/h1>&lt;p>The syntax of the &lt;code>sed&lt;/code> command is as follows:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-text" data-lang="text">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -options &amp;#39;operations&amp;#39; file-or-piped-input
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>&lt;code>sed&lt;/code> is a line editor; the operation commands here process text line by line&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="command-options">Command Options
&lt;/h2>&lt;p>Its main command options are as follows:&lt;/p>
&lt;table>
&lt;thead>
&lt;tr>
&lt;th style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Command Option&lt;/strong>&lt;/th>
&lt;th style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Meaning&lt;/strong>&lt;/th>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;/thead>
&lt;tbody>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>-n&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Suppress default output&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>-r&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Enable extended regular expressions&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>-i&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Edit the source file in place&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>-e&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Specify an operation command (enabled by default; multiple allowed)&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>-f&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Read operation commands from a file&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;/tbody>
&lt;/table>
&lt;h3 id="default-output">Default Output
&lt;/h3>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;/root/p&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># It actually displays all lines in the passwd file, with the line containing the root user shown twice&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -n &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;/root/p&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Adding the -n option suppresses sed&amp;#39;s own default output, so only the matching lines are shown via the p command&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;h3 id="modifying-the-original-file">Modifying the Original File
&lt;/h3>&lt;p>In real-world work, the &lt;code>-i&lt;/code> option is a fairly dangerous one, because it modifies the original file directly&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">3
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -i &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;/root/q&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># This operation leaves the passwd file containing only the root user line, because the q command exits immediately after a match&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># And sed&amp;#39;s output is then written directly back to the original file&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>A safer approach is to specify a backup file when using the &lt;code>-i&lt;/code> option, that is, to append a backup file suffix right after &lt;code>-i&lt;/code>&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -i.bak &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;/root/q&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Although this still modifies the passwd file, it creates a .bak backup before the change for easy recovery&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;h3 id="multiple-commands">Multiple Commands
&lt;/h3>&lt;p>The &lt;code>sed&lt;/code> command can take multiple operation commands. When there are several, you need to specify an &lt;code>-e&lt;/code> option for each one; if you use only a single command, the &lt;code>-e&lt;/code> can be omitted&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -e &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;=&amp;#39;&lt;/span> -e &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;/root/q&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># The = command prints the line number of the current line being processed&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>If you want to avoid repeating &lt;code>-e&lt;/code>, you can also combine commands with &lt;code>;&lt;/code>&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;=;/root/q&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Same effect as specifying multiple -e options&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;h2 id="operation-command-reference">Operation Command Reference
&lt;/h2>&lt;p>In the option introduction above, we&amp;rsquo;ve actually already used commands such as &lt;code>p&lt;/code> and &lt;code>q&lt;/code>.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>An operation command actually consists of two parts: the &lt;strong>address part&lt;/strong> and the &lt;strong>operation part&lt;/strong>:&lt;/p>
&lt;ul>
&lt;li>Address: &lt;code>sed&lt;/code> is a line-processing command, so addressing simply means specifying the lines to process&lt;/li>
&lt;li>Operation: the action to perform on the lines that match the addressing rule&lt;/li>
&lt;/ul>
&lt;h3 id="addressing-rules">Addressing Rules
&lt;/h3>&lt;table>
&lt;thead>
&lt;tr>
&lt;th style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Addressing Format&lt;/strong>&lt;/th>
&lt;th style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Meaning&lt;/strong>&lt;/th>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;/thead>
&lt;tbody>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Number&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Match a specified line number&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>m~n&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Start at line m and match with step n&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>$&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Match the last line&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>/regex/&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Match by regular expression&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>m,n&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">From line m to line n&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>m,+n&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">From line m, then match n more lines&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;/tbody>
&lt;/table>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt"> 1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 2
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 3
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 4
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 5
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 6
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 7
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 8
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 9
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">10
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">11
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">12
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">13
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">14
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">15
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">16
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">17
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -n &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;5p&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Display the content of line 5&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -n &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;2~3p&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Display lines starting from line 2 with a step of 3, i.e. lines 2, 5, 8, 11, 14, ...&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -n &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;$p&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Display the last line&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -n &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;/qiucao/p&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Display lines containing qiucao&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -n &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;5,8p&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Display the content from line 5 to line 8&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -n &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;5,+5p&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Display the content of lines 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;h3 id="operation-commands">Operation Commands
&lt;/h3>&lt;table>
&lt;thead>
&lt;tr>
&lt;th style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Operation Command&lt;/strong>&lt;/th>
&lt;th style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Meaning&lt;/strong>&lt;/th>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;/thead>
&lt;tbody>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>p&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Print&lt;/strong> matching lines&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>=&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Output the line number of matching lines&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>l&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Print matching lines and show &lt;strong>invisible characters&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>s&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Regular expression&lt;/strong> match and replace&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>c&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Replace the entire&lt;/strong> matching line&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>a&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Append&lt;/strong> content &lt;strong>after&lt;/strong> the matching line&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>i&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Insert&lt;/strong> content &lt;strong>before&lt;/strong> the matching line&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>d&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Delete&lt;/strong> matching lines&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>r&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Append content after the matching line (from a file)&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>w&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Write matching line content to a file&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>q&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Exit after a match, no further matching&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;/tbody>
&lt;/table>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt"> 1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 2
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 3
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 4
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 5
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 6
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 7
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 8
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt"> 9
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">10
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">11
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">12
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">13
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">14
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">15
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">16
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">17
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">18
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">19
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">20
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">21
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">22
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">23
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">24
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">25
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">26
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -n &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;=;5p&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Print all line numbers and display the content of line 5&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -n &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;=;5l&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Similar to the previous command, but the end-of-line marker ($) is also printed&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -r &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;=;s/^m\w*/test/g&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Replace all usernames starting with m with test; /g replaces all matches within a line, otherwise only the first is replaced&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;=;5c\测试一下&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Replace line 5 with &amp;#34;测试一下&amp;#34;&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;=;5a\测试一下&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Append a line after line 5&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;=;5i\测试一下&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Insert a line before line 5&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;=;5d&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Delete line 5&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;=;5r test.txt&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Read a line from test.txt and append it after line 5&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;=;5w test.txt&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Write the content of line 5 to test.txt&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;h1 id="pattern-space-and-hold-space">Pattern Space and Hold Space
&lt;/h1>&lt;p>The operation commands introduced above are mainly for within-line operations. Since we say &lt;code>sed&lt;/code> is actually an editor, it certainly also supports multi-line text editing operations, such as copying, pasting, and cutting whole lines.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>This brings us to the two memory spaces that &lt;code>sed&lt;/code> reserves during processing:&lt;/p>
&lt;ul>
&lt;li>&lt;strong>Pattern space&lt;/strong>: stores the content of the line currently being processed; after each line is handled, the processed content is placed in this space&lt;/li>
&lt;li>&lt;strong>Hold space&lt;/strong>: used to temporarily store data; it can be thought of as a clipboard-like cache for exchanging content between lines. Initially it contains only a single &lt;code>\n&lt;/code> newline character by default&lt;/li>
&lt;/ul>
&lt;table>
&lt;thead>
&lt;tr>
&lt;th style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Operation Command&lt;/strong>&lt;/th>
&lt;th style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>Usage&lt;/strong>&lt;/th>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;/thead>
&lt;tbody>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>h&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Copy the pattern space content to the hold space, replacing the original content (\n)&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>H&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Append the pattern space content to the hold space&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>g&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Copy the hold space content to the pattern space, overwriting the original content&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>G&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Append the hold space content after the current pattern space content&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">&lt;strong>x&lt;/strong>&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Swap the pattern space and hold space content&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">n&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Immediately read the next line and copy it to the pattern space&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">N&lt;/td>
&lt;td style="text-align: center">Immediately read the next line and append it to the current pattern space (often used to join lines)&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;/tbody>
&lt;/table>
&lt;h2 id="example">Example
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">3
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">4
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">5
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;=;2h;3H;5x;8G&amp;#39;&lt;/span> /etc/passwd
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># 2h copies line 2 to the hold space&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># 3H copies line 3 and appends it to the existing hold space&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># 5x swaps the current pattern space with the hold space, so the copied content replaces the original line 5&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># 8G appends the hold space content (originally the pattern space content of line 5) after the current pattern space, i.e. after line 8&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>&lt;img src="https://chengxiaqiucao.github.io/post/061-linux%E6%96%87%E6%9C%AC%E5%A4%84%E7%90%86%E4%B8%89%E5%89%91%E5%AE%A2-sed/Pasted-20250313_hu_17064a38a141561a.webp"
width="1024"
