>
&lt;/p>
&lt;h1 id="common-practical-sed-examples">Common Practical sed Examples
&lt;/h1>&lt;h2 id="1-delete-blank-lines">1. Delete Blank Lines
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -i &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;/^\s*$/d&amp;#39;&lt;/span> test.txt
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Match lines containing only whitespace characters with a regular expression and delete them&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;h2 id="2-add-line-numbers-to-a-file">2. Add Line Numbers to a File
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">3
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;=&amp;#39;&lt;/span> test.txt &lt;span class="p">|&lt;/span> sed &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;N;s/\n/\t/&amp;#39;&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># First use sed to get the line numbers, added as a line before each line&amp;#39;s default output&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Pipe the result to a second sed command, use the N command to merge the two lines, and replace the newline with a tab&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;h2 id="3-extract-all-image-links">3. Extract All Image Links
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -nr &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;s/.*src=&amp;#34;(.*\.png).*/\1/p&amp;#39;&lt;/span> source.html
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># A regular expression matches the image link; a capture group saves the path and replaces the entire original line&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;h2 id="4-extract-error-lines-from-a-log">4. Extract Error Lines from a Log
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sed -n &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;/ERROR/{=;p}&amp;#39;&lt;/span> app.log
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># Match lines in the log containing ERROR and print them along with their line numbers&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;h1 id="conclusion">Conclusion
&lt;/h1>&lt;p>The above is a systematic overview of the &lt;code>sed&lt;/code> command from the Linux Text Processing Trio. A summary of the &lt;code>awk&lt;/code> command will follow in a future article! For more extended testing skills, feel free to follow Qiucao&amp;rsquo;s systematic improvement course and reply with &lt;code>outline&lt;/code> to get the detailed catalog.&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>A Guide to grep, One of the Linux Text Processing Trio</title><link>https://chengxiaqiucao.github.io/en/post/060-linux%E6%96%87%E6%9C%AC%E5%A4%84%E7%90%86%E4%B8%89%E5%89%91%E5%AE%A2-grep/</link><pubDate>Thu, 20 Feb 2025 14:02:28 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://chengxiaqiucao.github.io/en/post/060-linux%E6%96%87%E6%9C%AC%E5%A4%84%E7%90%86%E4%B8%89%E5%89%91%E5%AE%A2-grep/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://chengxiaqiucao.github.io/post/060-linux%E6%96%87%E6%9C%AC%E5%A4%84%E7%90%86%E4%B8%89%E5%89%91%E5%AE%A2-grep/Pasted-20250310.webp" alt="Featured image of post A Guide to grep, One of the Linux Text Processing Trio" />&lt;p>&lt;img src="https://chengxiaqiucao.github.io/post/060-linux%E6%96%87%E6%9C%AC%E5%A4%84%E7%90%86%E4%B8%89%E5%89%91%E5%AE%A2-grep/Pasted-20250310_hu_76c8e40a9b25c995.webp"
