&lt;/p>
&lt;h1 id="preface">Preface
&lt;/h1>&lt;p>&lt;code>grep&lt;/code> is one of the Linux trio (&lt;code>grep&lt;/code>, &lt;code>sed&lt;/code>, &lt;code>awk&lt;/code>), used to search for specific text patterns in files. It is not only powerful but also very flexible, capable of handling a wide variety of text-search needs. Below we walk through the detailed usage of the &lt;code>grep&lt;/code> command and its advanced examples.&lt;/p>
&lt;h1 id="basic-usage">Basic Usage
&lt;/h1>&lt;h2 id="basic-search">Basic Search
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern&amp;#34;&lt;/span> filename
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Used to search the &lt;code>filename&lt;/code> file for lines containing &lt;code>pattern&lt;/code> and print them out.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="ignoring-case">Ignoring Case
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -i &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern&amp;#34;&lt;/span> filename
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Use the &lt;code>-i&lt;/code> option to ignore case.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="recursive-search">Recursive Search
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -r &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern&amp;#34;&lt;/span> directory
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Recursively search the contents of files containing &lt;code>pattern&lt;/code> in the &lt;code>directory&lt;/code> and its subdirectories.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="showing-line-numbers">Showing Line Numbers
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -n &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern&amp;#34;&lt;/span> filename
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Use the &lt;code>-n&lt;/code> option to show the line numbers of matching lines.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="inverted-search-show-non-matching-lines">Inverted Search (Show Non-Matching Lines)
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -v &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern&amp;#34;&lt;/span> filename
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Use the &lt;code>-v&lt;/code> option to show lines that do not contain &lt;code>pattern&lt;/code>.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="showing-file-names">Showing File Names
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern&amp;#34;&lt;/span> file1 file2 file3
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>When searching multiple files, the matching file names are shown by default. Use &lt;code>-H&lt;/code> to force showing file names, and use &lt;code>-h&lt;/code> to hide them.&lt;/p>
&lt;h1 id="advanced-usage">Advanced Usage
&lt;/h1>&lt;h2 id="using-regular-expressions">Using Regular Expressions
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -E &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern1|pattern2&amp;#34;&lt;/span> filename
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Use the &lt;code>-E&lt;/code> option to support extended regular expressions, where &lt;code>|&lt;/code> means the OR operation.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="counting-matching-lines">Counting Matching Lines
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -c &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern&amp;#34;&lt;/span> filename
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Use the &lt;code>-c&lt;/code> option to count the number of matching lines.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="showing-only-the-matched-part">Showing Only the Matched Part
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -o &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern&amp;#34;&lt;/span> filename
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Use the &lt;code>-o&lt;/code> option to show only the matched part instead of the entire line.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="showing-lines-before-and-after">Showing Lines Before and After
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">2
&lt;/span>&lt;span class="lnt">3
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -A &lt;span class="m">3&lt;/span> &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern&amp;#34;&lt;/span> filename &lt;span class="c1"># Show the matching line and the 3 lines after it&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -B &lt;span class="m">3&lt;/span> &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern&amp;#34;&lt;/span> filename &lt;span class="c1"># Show the matching line and the 3 lines before it&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -C &lt;span class="m">3&lt;/span> &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern&amp;#34;&lt;/span> filename &lt;span class="c1"># Show the matching line and 3 lines before and after it&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>The &lt;code>-A&lt;/code>, &lt;code>-B&lt;/code>, and &lt;code>-C&lt;/code> options are used to show a specified number of lines after, before, or both before and after the matching line, respectively.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="reading-data-from-standard-input">Reading Data from Standard Input
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="nb">echo&lt;/span> &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;some text&amp;#34;&lt;/span> &lt;span class="p">|&lt;/span> grep &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern&amp;#34;&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>&lt;code>grep&lt;/code> can read data from standard input, which is very useful in pipeline operations.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="searching-with-fixed-strings-improving-performance">Searching with Fixed Strings (Improving Performance)
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -F &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;fixed_string&amp;#34;&lt;/span> filename
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Use the &lt;code>-F&lt;/code> option to treat &lt;code>pattern&lt;/code> as a fixed string rather than a regular expression, which can improve search performance.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="excluding-files">Excluding Files
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -r --exclude&lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span>&lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;*.log&amp;#34;&lt;/span> &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;pattern&amp;#34;&lt;/span> directory
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Use the &lt;code>--exclude&lt;/code> option to exclude files of a certain type.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="using-perl-compatible-regular-expressions">Using Perl-Compatible Regular Expressions
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -P &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;\d+&amp;#34;&lt;/span> filename
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Use the &lt;code>-P&lt;/code> option to enable Perl-compatible regular expressions, where &lt;code>\d+&lt;/code> means one or more digits.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="zero-width-assertions">Zero-Width Assertions
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -P &lt;span class="s1">&amp;#39;(?&amp;lt;=foo)bar&amp;#39;&lt;/span> filename
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Use Perl-compatible regular expressions to support zero-width assertions; for example, &lt;code>(?&amp;lt;=foo)bar&lt;/code> matches a &lt;code>bar&lt;/code> that is preceded by &lt;code>foo&lt;/code>.&lt;/p>
&lt;h1 id="combined-usage-examples">Combined Usage Examples
&lt;/h1>&lt;h2 id="finding-files-of-a-specific-type-in-a-specific-directory-and-searching-their-contents">Finding Files of a Specific Type in a Specific Directory and Searching Their Contents
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -r --include&lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span>&lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;*.py&amp;#34;&lt;/span> &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;import&amp;#34;&lt;/span> /path/to/directory
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Recursively search all &lt;code>.py&lt;/code> files in the &lt;code>/path/to/directory&lt;/code> directory for lines containing &lt;code>import&lt;/code>.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="finding-and-showing-matching-lines-with-two-lines-before-and-after">Finding and Showing Matching Lines with Two Lines Before and After
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -C &lt;span class="m">2&lt;/span> &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;error&amp;#34;&lt;/span> /var/log/syslog
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Find lines containing &lt;code>error&lt;/code> in the &lt;code>/var/log/syslog&lt;/code> file and show the two lines before and after them.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="counting-the-number-of-files-containing-a-specific-word">Counting the Number of Files Containing a Specific Word
&lt;/h2>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;div class="chroma">
&lt;table class="lntable">&lt;tr>&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code>&lt;span class="lnt">1
&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>
&lt;td class="lntd">
&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">grep -rl &lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;word&amp;#34;&lt;/span> /path/to/directory &lt;span class="p">|&lt;/span> wc -l
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/td>&lt;/tr>&lt;/table>
&lt;/div>
&lt;/div>&lt;p>Recursively search the &lt;code>/path/to/directory&lt;/code> directory for files containing &lt;code>word&lt;/code>, and count the number of files.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion
&lt;/h2>&lt;p>Through these examples, you can appreciate the power and flexibility of &lt;code>grep&lt;/code>, which also makes it one of the most practical text-processing tools in Linux.&lt;/p></description></item></channel></rss>